"Nonlinear Principal Components and Long-run Implications of Multivariate Diffusions (https://arxiv.org/abs/0908.0547)\nciteturn964academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.2 years ago; Title: Nonlinear Principal Components and Long-run Implications of Multivariate DiffusionsAuthors: Xioahong Chen, Lars Peter Hansen, Jose Scheinkman ... Moreover, the NPCs behave as scalar autoregressions with heteroskedastic innovations; this supports semiparametric identification and estimation of a multivariate reversible diffusion process and tests of the overidentifying restrictions implied by such a process from low frequency data.\nTitle: Nonlinear Principal Components and Long-run Implications of Multivariate Diffusions\nAuthors: Xioahong Chen, Lars Peter Hansen, Jose Scheinkman\nDate: Tue Aug  4 21:27:28 2009\n\nWe investigate a method for extracting nonlinear principal components (NPCs). These NPCs maximize variation subject to smoothness and orthogonality constraints; but we allow for a general class of constraints and multivariate probability densities, including densities without compact support and even densities with algebraic tails. We provide primitive sufficient conditions for the existence of these NPCs. By exploiting the theory of continuous-time, reversible Markov diffusion processes, we give a different interpretation of these NPCs and the smoothness constraints. When the diffusion matrix is used to enforce smoothness, the NPCs maximize long-run variation relative to the overall variation subject to orthogonality constraints. Moreover, the NPCs behave as scalar autoregressions with heteroskedastic innovations; this supports semiparametric identification and estimation of a multivariate reversible diffusion process and tests of the overidentifying restrictions implied by such a process from low frequency data. We also explore implications for stationary, possibly non-reversible diffusion processes. Finally, we suggest a sieve method to estimate the NPCs from discretely-sampled data.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectral methods for identifying scalar diffusions - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0304407697001073)\nciteturn964search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 28.1 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; The drift and diffusion coefficients of stationary, scalar diffusions are identifiable from discrete-time data without parametric restrictions (e.g., see Hansen and Scheinkman, 1995). ... In particular, exact identification can be achieved using the parameterization of a diffusion proposed in Section 5. ... ### Harmonic Analysis and the Theory of Probability ...   * Conley, T., Hansen, L.P., Luttmer, E.G.J., Scheinkman, J., 1997.\n\n## Journal of Econometrics\n\nVolume 86, Issue 1, September 1998, Pages 1-32\n\n# Spectral methods for identifying scalar diffusions\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/S0304-4076(97)00107-3Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis paper shows how to identify nonparametrically scalar stationary diffusions from discrete-time data. The local evolution of the diffusion is characterized by a drift and diffusion coefficient along with the specification of boundary behavior. We recover this local evolution from two objects that can be inferred directly from discrete-time data: the stationary density and a conveniently chosen eigenvalue–eigenfunction pair of the conditional expectation operator over a unit interval of time. This construction also lends itself to a spectral characterization of the over-identifying restrictions implied by a scalar diffusion model of a discrete-time Markov process.\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe drift and diffusion coefficients of stationary, scalar diffusions are identifiable from discrete-time data without parametric restrictions (e.g., see Hansen and Scheinkman, 1995). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPII: S0304-4076(97)00107-3 (https://larspeterhansen.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/Spectral-Methods-for-Identifying-Scalar-Diffusions.pdf)\nciteturn964search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Lars Peter Hansenᵃ,*, José Alexandre Scheinkmanᵃ, Nizar Touziᵇ ... © 1998 Elsevier Science S.A. ... *Keywords:* Diffusion; Identification; Embeddability; Continuous-time models in finance; Spectral decomposition ... The drift and diffusion coefficients of stationary, scalar diffusions are identifiable from discrete-time data without parametric restrictions (e.g., see Hansen and Scheinkman, 1995).\nJournal of Econometrics 86 (1998) 1–32\n\nSpectral methods for identifying scalar diffusions\n\nLars Peter Hansenᵃ,*, José Alexandre Scheinkmanᵃ, Nizar Touziᵇ\n\nᵃ Department of Economics, The University of Chicago, 1126 East 59th Street, Chicago, IL 60637, USA  \nᵇ Crest, USA\n\nReceived 1 January 1996; received in revised form 1 March 1997\n\n**Abstract**\n\nThis paper shows how to identify nonparametrically scalar stationary diffusions from discrete-time data. The local evolution of the diffusion is characterized by a drift and diffusion coefficient along with the specification of boundary behavior. We recover this local evolution from two objects that can be inferred directly from discrete-time data: the stationary density and a conveniently chosen eigenvalue–eigenfunction pair of the conditional expectation operator over a unit interval of time. This construction also lends itself to a spectral characterization of the over-identifying restrictions implied by a scalar diffusion model of a discrete-time Markov process. © 1998 Elsevier Science S.A. All rights reserved.\n\n*JEL classification:* C4\n\n*Keywords:* Diffusion; Identification; Embeddability; Continuous-time models in finance; Spectral decomposition\n\n**1. Introduction**\n\nThe drift and diffusion coefficients of stationary, scalar diffusions are identifiable from discrete-time data without parametric restrictions (e.g., see Hansen and Scheinkman, 1995). In this paper we explore this identification more fully studying the relationship between two operators. One operator is the infinitesimal generator which captures the local evolution of the Markov process. This operator is a second-order differential operator with coefficients given by the\n\n* Corresponding author. E-mail: aajlphan@hilbert.spcuchicago.edu\n\n0304-4076/98/$19.00 © 1998 Elsevier Science S.A. All rights reserved.  \nPII S0304-4076(97)00107-3--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Annals of Statistics (https://larspeterhansen.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/Nonlinear-Principal-Components-and-Long-Run-Implications-of-Multivariate-Diffusions.pdf)\nciteturn964search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; BY XIAOHONG CHEN2, LARS PETER HANSEN3 AND JOSÉ SCHEINKMAN4 ... supports semiparametric identiﬁcation and estimation of a multivariate re- ... sieve method to estimate the NPCs from discretely-sampled data. ... Nonlinear principal components, multivariate diffusion, quadratic form,\nThe Annals of Statistics\n2009, Vol. 37, No. 6B, 4279–4312\nDOI: 10.1214/09-AOS706\n© Institute of Mathematical Statistics, 2009\nNONLINEAR PRINCIPAL COMPONENTS AND LONG-RUN\nIMPLICATIONS OF MULTIVARIATE DIFFUSIONS1\nBY XIAOHONG CHEN2, LARS PETER HANSEN3 AND JOSÉ SCHEINKMAN4\nYale University, University of Chicago and Princeton University\nWe investigate a method for extracting nonlinear principal components\n(NPCs). These NPCs maximize variation subject to smoothness and orthog-\nonality constraints; but we allow for a general class of constraints and multi-\nvariate probability densities, including densities without compact support and\neven densities with algebraic tails. We provide primitive sufﬁcient conditions\nfor the existence of these NPCs. By exploiting the theory of continuous-time,\nreversible Markov diffusion processes, we give a different interpretation of\nthese NPCs and the smoothness constraints. When the diffusion matrix is\nused to enforce smoothness, the NPCs maximize long-run variation relative\nto the overall variation subject to orthogonality constraints. Moreover, the\nNPCs behave as scalar autoregressions with heteroskedastic innovations; this\nsupports semiparametric identiﬁcation and estimation of a multivariate re-\nversible diffusion process and tests of the overidentifying restrictions implied\nby such a process from low-frequency data. We also explore implications for\nstationary, possibly nonreversible diffusion processes. Finally, we suggest a\nsieve method to estimate the NPCs from discretely-sampled data.\n1. Introduction.\nPrincipal components are functions of the data that capture\nmaximal variation in some sense. Often they are restricted to be linear functions of\nthe underlying data as in original analyses of Pearson [27] and Hotelling [23]. In\nthis paper we study the extraction of nonlinear principal components (NPCs) us-\ning information encoded in the probability density of the data. Formally, the NPCs\nmaximize variation subject to orthogonality and smoothness constraints where\nsmoothness constraints are enforced by a quadratic form f expressed in terms\nof the gradients of functions. Speciﬁcally, the quadratic form is\n\u0002\nf (φ,ψ) = 1\n2\nx∈\u0004 ∇φ(x)′\u0005(x)∇ψ(x)q(x)dx,\nReceived December 2008.\n1The title of an earlier version of this paper is Principal Components and the Long Run.\n2Supported by National Science Foundation under Award Number SES0631613.\n3Supported by National Science Foundation under Award Number SES0519372.\n4Supported\nby\nNational\nScience\nFoundation\nunder\nAward\nNumbers\nSES0350770\nand\nSES0718407.\nAMS 2000 subject classiﬁcations. Primary 62H25, 47D07; secondary 35P05.\nKey words and phrases. Nonlinear principal components, multivariate diffusion, quadratic form,\nconditional expectations operator, low-frequency data.\n4279--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Nonlinear Principal Components and Long-run Implications of Multivariate Diffusions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/45865899_Nonlinear_Principal_Components_and_Long-run_Implications_of_Multivariate_Diffusions)\nciteturn964search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.2 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Moreover, the NPCs behave as scalar autoregressions with heteroskedastic innovations; this supports semiparametric identification and estimation of a multivariate reversible diffusion process and tests of the overidentifying restrictions implied by such a process from low frequency data. ... Finally, we suggest a sieve method to estimate the NPCs from discretely-sampled data. ... Keywords and phrases: nonlinear principal components, multivariate diﬀusion, ... 2 CHEN, HANSEN AND SCHEINKMAN ... The j th nonlinear principal component ψ j for j≥1\n\n# Nonlinear Principal Components and Long-run Implications of Multivariate Diffusions\n\n  * August 2009\n  * The Annals of Statistics 37(6)\n\nDOI:10.1214/09-AOS706\n\nAuthors:\n\nXiaohong Chen\n\n  * Yale University\n\nLars Peter Hansen\n\n  * University of Chicago\n\nJose Scheinkman\n\n  * Columbia University\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe investigate a method for extracting nonlinear principal components (NPCs). These NPCs maximize variation subject to smoothness and orthogonality constraints; but we allow for a general class of constraints and multivariate probability densities, including densities without compact support and even densities with algebraic tails. We provide primitive sufficient conditions for the existence of these NPCs. By exploiting the theory of continuous-time, reversible Markov diffusion processes, we give a different interpretation of these NPCs and the smoothness constraints. \nSubmitted to the Annals of Statistics\n\nNONLINEAR PRINCIPAL COMPONENTS AND\n\nLONG-RUN IMPLICATIONS OF MULTIVARIATE\n\nDIFFUSIONS∗\n\nBy Xiaohong Chen†, Lars Peter Hansen‡and Jos´\n\ne Scheinkman§\n\nYale University, University of Chicago and Princeton University\n\nWe investigate a method for extracting nonlinear principal com-\n\nponents (NPCs). These NPCs maximize variation subject to smooth-\n\nness and orthogonality constraints; but we allow for a general class\n\nof constraints and multivariate probability densities, including densi-\n\nties without compact support and even densities with algebraic tails.\n\nWe provide primitive suﬃcient conditions for the existence of these\n\nNPCs. By exploiting the theory of continuous-time, reversible Markov\n\ndiﬀusion processes, we give a diﬀerent interpretation of these NPCs\n\nand the smoothness constraints. When the diﬀusion matrix is used to\n\nenforce smoothness, the NPCs maximize long-run variation relative\n\nto the overall variation subject to orthogonality constraints. More-\nKeywords and phrases: nonlinear principal components, multivariate diﬀusion,\n\nquadratic form, conditional expectations operator, low frequency data\n\n1\n\n2 CHEN, HANSEN AND SCHEINKMAN\n\ndensity of the data. Formally, the NPCs maximize variation subject to or-\n\nthogonality and smoothness constraints where smoothness constraints are\n\nenforced by a quadratic form f expressed in terms of the gradients of func-\n\ntions. Speciﬁcally, the quadratic form is\n\nf(φ, ψ) = 1\n\n2 Z x∈Ω∇φ(x)′Σ(x)∇ψ(x)q(x)dx\n\nwhere ∇denotes the (weak) gradient operator, Ω is the state space, Σ is a\n\nstate-dependent positive-deﬁnite matrix, and q is the invariant density of a\n\nstrictly stationary ergodic data {x i}T\n\ni=1.\n\nAlternatively, NPCs are solutions to approximation problems. Suppose\n\nwe wish to form the best ﬁnite-dimensional least squares approximation to\n\nan inﬁnite-dimensional space of smooth functions, where we use the form\n\nf to limit the class of functions to be approximated. In a sense that we\n\nmake formal, a ﬁnite number of NPCs solves this problem. \nproblem:\n\n(7.1) V T b\n\na T=b\n\nδ T W T b\n\na T\n\nwhere b\n\na T is a generalized eigenvector and b\n\nδ T a generalized eigenvalue. Since\n\nW T is positive deﬁnite, we may apply the Cholesky decomposition to trans-\n\nform this generalized eigenvector problem into a standard eigenvector prob-\n\nlem.\n\nAssociated with each eigenvector solution to (7.1), is an eigenfunction\n\nformed by multiplying the coeﬃcient entries of the eigenvector by the sieve\n\nbasis functions. We have constructed this sample problem so that one of the\n\napproximating eigenfunctions will be constant whenever there is a nonzero\n\nconstant function in H m, and the associated eigenvalue is zero.3\n\n8. Conclusions and related literature. We have studied NPCs from\n\nmultiple vantage points. We have explored their role in capturing variation\n\nsubject to smoothness constraints and their role in capturing long-run vari-\n\nation in time series modeling. We have also considered their use in approxi-\n\nmation where the smoothness constraints limit the family of functions to be\n\napproximated.\n\nWe also used multivariate Markov diﬀusions as data generating devices to\n\ninterpret our NPCs. These NPCs are eigenfunctions of conditional expecta-\n\ntion operators when the Markov process is reversible and hence imply con-\n\nditional moment restrictions. Our analysis expands on the result of Hansen\n\n3 For reversible diﬀusions we could instead approximate the NPCs nonparametrically\n\nby maximizing autocorrelations. For scalar diﬀusion models, this method has been already\n\nconsidered in Chen et al. [9] and Gobet et al. [19]. Both papers used a wavelet sieve as\n\nthe approximating space.\n\nNONLINEAR PRINCIPAL COMPONENTS AND THE LONG RUN 31\n\nand Scheinkman [21] that reversible diﬀusions can be identiﬁed nonpara-\n\nmetrically from discrete-time low frequency stationary observations.\n\nFor more general diﬀusions, these NPC are orthogonal and have orthog-\n\n\npal components motivated in part by long-run implications of multivariate\n\nMarkov diﬀusions. This is a natural ﬁrst step. Inferential issues, while cru-\n\ncial, are beyond the scope of this paper. Formalizing statistical comparisons\n\nof models and data in a multivariate setting is an obvious next step, sup-\n\nported by either parametric, semiparametric or nonparametric estimation.\n\nThere are a number of recent statistical results on estimation and inference\n\nof functional principal components of covariance operators associated with\n\ni.i.d. or longitudinal sample of curves. See e.g., Silverman [32], Ramsay and\n\nSilverman [28], Hall et al. [20], Benko et al. [4] and Zhou et al. [35]. These\n\n32 CHEN, HANSEN AND SCHEINKMAN\n\nexisting results can in principle be modiﬁed to establish asymptotic proper-\n\nties of our estimated NPCs from discrete-time low frequency realizations of\n\nan underlying multivariate Markov diﬀusion model.\n\nNONLINEAR PRINCIPAL COMPONENTS AND THE LONG RUN 33\n\nAPPENDIX A: PROOFS\n\nProof of Claim 2.2. In solving the maximization component of the\n\nproblem, ﬁrst limit the φ’s to be in H N but orthogonal to ˆ\n\nH. This can only\n\nreduce maximized value. The space of such φ’s contains more than just the\n\nzero element because H N has N+ 1 dimensions. Write φ as: φ=P N\n\nj=0 r j ψ j.\n\nSince P roj (φ|ˆ\n\nH) = 0, the objective can be expressed as: P N\n\nj=0(r j)2 λ j. The\n\nconstraint set implies that\n\nN\n\nX\n\nj=0\n\n(r j)2≤1\n\nbecause f(ψ j, ψ ℓ) =< ψ j, ψ ℓ>= 0 for j 6=ℓ. While the coeﬃcients r j cannot\n\nbe freely chosen (φ must be orthogonal to ˆ\n\nH), they can be scaled so that the\n\nconstraint is satisﬁed with equality. Since the sequence of λ j’s is decreasing,\n\nthe maximized objective must be no less than λ N.\n\nProof of Claim 2.3. Write φ as: φ=P roj(φ|H N−1) + ϕ where ϕ is in\n\nH⊥\n\nN−1. Write:\n\nP roj (φ|H N−1) =\n\nN−1\n\nX\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrincipal Components - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics (https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/economics-econometrics-and-finance/principal-components)\nciteturn964search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Scheinkman ... These components are a nonlinear counterpart to the more familiar principal component analysis of covariance matrices advocated by Pearson (1901). ... As argued by Chen et al. (2008) they are equally valuable in the analysis of time series data. ... The Markov diffusion model provides this link.\n\nChapter\n\n## Operator Methods for Continuous-Time Markov Processes\n\n2010, Handbook of Financial Econometrics Tools and TechniquesYacine Aït-Sahalia, ... José A. Scheinkman\n\n### 4.2. Principal Components\n\nGiven two quadratic forms, we define the functional versions of principal components.\n\nDefinition 6 Nonlinear principal components are functions ψ_{j}, j -1,2...… that solve:\n\n\\underset{\\varphi }{max}{f}_{1}\\left(\\varphi ,\\varphi \\right)\n\nsubject to\n\n\\begin{array}{lll}\\hfill {f}_{2}\\left(\\varphi ,\\varphi \\right)& =\\hfill & 1\\hfill \\\\ \\hfill {f}_{1}\\left(\\varphi ,{\\psi }_{s}\\right)& =\\hfill & 0,\\hspace{0.17em}\\hspace{0.17em}s=0,\\dots ,\\hspace{0.17em}\\hspace{0.17em}j-1\\hfill \\end{array}\n\nwhere ψ_{0} is initialized to be the constant function one.\n\nThis definition follows Chen et al. (2008) and is a direct extension of that used by Salinelli (1998) for i.i.d. data. In the case of a diffusion specification, form f_{2} is given by (4.2) and induces a quadratic smoothness penalty. Principal components maximize variation subject to a smoothness constraint and orthogonality. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNonlinear Principal Components and Long-run Implications of Multivariate Diffusions (https://home.uchicago.edu/~lhansen/pclongfinal.pdf)\nciteturn964search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.2 years ago; NONLINEAR PRINCIPAL COMPONENTS AND THE LONG RUN ... cations of multivariate nonlinear diﬀusion models. ... been suggested previously by Demoura [15], Hansen and Scheinkman [21], ... [17], Chen et al. ... There are a number of recent statistical results on estimation and inference\nNONLINEAR PRINCIPAL COMPONENTS AND THE LONG RUN\n31\nand Scheinkman [21] that reversible diﬀusions can be identiﬁed nonpara-\nmetrically from discrete-time low frequency stationary observations.\nFor more general diﬀusions, these NPC are orthogonal and have orthog-\nonal innovations analogous to those from the canonical analysis of Box and\nTiao [7] for linear multiple time series models. (See also Pan and Yao [26].)\nThus our NPC construction provides a convenient way to summarize impli-\ncations of multivariate nonlinear diﬀusion models. Given the nonlinearity in\nthe state variables, it is a nontrivial task to infer the global dynamics, and\nin particular the long-run behavior from this local speciﬁcation based on low\nfrequency data. Our characterization of NPCs oﬀers a way to characterize\nfeatures of the implied time series that are typically disguised from the local\ndynamics. While we featured diﬀusion processes, more general processes in-\ncluding processes with jumps can be accommodated by expanding the types\nof forms that are considered.\nThe idea of using eigenfunctions of conditional expectation operators for\nestimation and testing of Markov processes based on low frequency data has\nbeen suggested previously by Demoura [15], Hansen and Scheinkman [21],\nKessler and Sorensen [24], Hansen et al. [22], Florens et al. [17], Chen et al. [9]\nand Gobet et al. [19]. In particular, Kessler and Sorensen [24] use eigenfunc-\ntions to construct quasi-optimal estimators of parametric scalar diﬀusion\nmodels of the drift and diﬀusion coeﬃcients from discrete-time data in the\nspecial case in which the functional forms of eigenfunctions are known a pri-\nori. Hansen and Scheinkman [21], Hansen et al. [22], Chen et al. [9], Gobet\net al. [19] and Darolles et al. [11] study semiparametric and nonparametric\nidentiﬁcation and over-identiﬁcation based on an eigenfunction extraction\nthat is closely related to the one analyzed here; see Fan [16] for a recent\nreview. This previous literature focuses primarily on scalar diﬀusion models\nand in some cases to scalar diﬀusions on compact state spaces with reﬂec-\ntive boundaries. Our analysis of Markov diﬀusions extends to multivariate\nsettings applicable to processes without attracting barriers.\nIn this paper we have characterized a particular type of functional princi-\npal components motivated in part by long-run implications of multivariate\nMarkov diﬀusions. This is a natural ﬁrst step. Inferential issues, while cru-\ncial, are beyond the scope of this paper. Formalizing statistical comparisons\nof models and data in a multivariate setting is an obvious next step, sup-\nported by either parametric, semiparametric or nonparametric estimation.\nThere are a number of recent statistical results on estimation and inference\nof functional principal components of covariance operators associated with\ni.i.d. or longitudinal sample of curves. See e.g., Silverman [32], Ramsay and\nSilverman [28], Hall et al. [20], Benko et al. [4] and Zhou et al. [35]. These--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn analysis of Hansen–Scheinkman moment estimators for discretely and randomly sampled diffusions - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S030440760700228X)\nciteturn964search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Let the parameter vector be written as ${\\beta }^{\\prime }=(\\theta ,\\gamma )$ where \\theta $\\theta $ is the parameter entering the drift function and \\gamma $\\gamma $ that entering the diffusion function. ... We start by briefly summarizing this theory in a special case (consistent estimators, exact identification, restrictive choice of moment functions, scalar parameters) that will be sufficient for its application to the HS moment conditions. ... Hansen et al.### Identification of scalar diffusions using eigenvectors\n\n  * L.P. Hansen et al.\n\n### Identification of scalar diffusions using eigenvectors\n\n### Journal of Econometrics\n\n(1998)\n\n  * Y. Aı¨t-Sahalia\n\n### Testing continuous-time models of the spot interest rate\n\n### Review of Financial Studies\n\n(1996)\n\n  * Y. Aı¨t-Sahalia\n\n### Maximum-likelihood estimation of discretely-sampled diffusions: a closed-form approximation approach\n\n### Econometrica\n\n(2002)\n\n  * Y. Aı¨t-Sahalia et al.\n\n### The effects of random and discrete sampling when estimating continuous-time diffusions\n\n### Econometrica\n\n(2003)\n\n  * Y. Aı¨t-Sahalia et al.\n\n### Estimators of diffusions with randomly spaced discrete observations: a general theory\n\n### The Annals of Statistics\n\n(2004)\n\n  * T.G. Conley et al.\n\n### Short-term interest rates as subordinated diffusions\n\n### Review of Financial Studies\n\n(1997)\n\n  * D. Duffie et al.\n\n### Estimation of continuous-time Markov processes sampled at random times\n\n### Econometrica\n\n(2004)\n\nThere are more references available in the full text version of this article.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPrincipal Components and Long Run Implications of Multivariate Diffusions (https://home.uchicago.edu/~lhansen/pclong5.pdf)\nciteturn964search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.9 years ago; Chen, Hansen and Scheinkman/Principal Components and the Long Run ... Our construction of nonlinear principal components supports the estimationand testing of multivariate Markov diﬀusion models.\nChen, Hansen and Scheinkman/Principal Components and the Long Run\n10\nDeﬁnition 3.1. The function ψj is the jth nonlinear principal component for\nj ≥0 if ψj solves:\nmin\nφ f(φ, φ)\nsubject to\n< φ, φ >\n=\n1\n< ψs, φ >\n=\n0, s = 0, ..., j −1.\nThus the principal components are extracting by making the local forecast er-\nror (appropriately scaled) small for functions with unit second moments plus\northogonality. It is a continuous-time counterpart to one minus the R2 in a fore-\ncasting regression. Recall that the principal components satisfy < ψj, ψk >= 0\nand f(ψj, ψk) = 0 for j ̸= k. These properties are nonlinear counterparts to the\ncanonical components in the extraction of Box and Tiao (1977). Box and Tiao\n(1977) show that their canonical analysis produces k components series which\nare ordered from least predictable to most predictable, are contemporaneously\nuncorrelated, and have unpredictable components which are contemporane-\nously uncorrelated. In verifying that third property, notice that the continuous\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA martingale approach for testing diffusion models based on infinitesimal operator - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304407610002472)\nciteturn964search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.4 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Several nice advantages over the existing studies are expected to come up with the properties of the infinitesimal operator based martingale characterization, for example, the complete identification of the diffusion process unlike Hansen and Scheinkman’s (1995) identification up to scale, the closed-form expressions unlike the eigenfunctions used in Kessler and Sorensen (1996) and Hansen et al. (1998), and convenient applications to multivariate diffusions unlike Kanaya (2007) which is applicable only for univariate cases.\n\n## Journal of Econometrics\n\nVolume 162, Issue 2, June 2011, Pages 189-212\n\n# A martingale approach for testing diffusion models based on infinitesimal operator☆\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.jeconom.2010.12.005Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nI develop an omnibus specification test for diffusion models based on the infinitesimal operator. The infinitesimal operator based identification of the diffusion process is equivalent to a “martingale hypothesis” for the processes obtained by a transformation of the original diffusion model. My test procedure is then constructed by checking the “martingale hypothesis” via a multivariate generalized spectral derivative based approach that delivers a asymptotical null distribution for the test statistic. The infinitesimal operator of the diffusion process is a closed-form function of drift and diffusion terms. Consequently, my test procedure covers both univariate and multivariate diffusion models in a unified framework and is particularly convenient for the multivariate case. (1992), Cox et al. (1985) and Vasicek (1977). However, model misspecification may yield misleading conclusions about the dynamics of the process and result in large errors in pricing, hedging and risk management. Therefore, the development of reliable specification tests for diffusion models is necessary to tackle such problems.\n\nIn this study, I develop an omnibus test for the specification of diffusion models based on the infinitesimal operator which is a complete characterization of the whole dynamics of the process alternative to transition density used by Ait-Sahalia et al. (2009), Chen et al. (2008) and Hong and Li (2005). Through the celebrated “martingale problem” developed by Stroock and Varadhan (1969), the infinitesimal operator based martingale characterization of a diffusion process is obtained such that the identification of the diffusion process is equivalent to a “martingale hypothesis” for the processes transformed from the original diffusion process. I then check the “martingale hypothesis” via a multivariate generalized spectral derivative approach, which is an extension of Hong (1999) for univariate time series processes. Such a test is particularly powerful against alternatives with zero autocorrelation but a nonzero conditional mean and has a convenient one-sided asymptotic distribution. The infinitesimal operator of the diffusion process enjoys the nice property of being a closed-form expression of drift and diffusion terms. This makes my test procedure feature many good properties which will be discussed in the following.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIdentification of continuous, spatiotemporal systems | Phys. Rev. E (https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.57.2820)\nciteturn964search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 28.6 years ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; # Identification of continuous, spatiotemporal systems ... We present a method for the identification of continuous, spatiotemporal dynamics from experimental data.We use a model in the form of a partial differential equation and formulate an optimization problem for its estimation from data.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIdentification of a linear continuous time stochastic model of the heat dynamics of a greenhouse - Aarhus University (https://pure.au.dk/portal/en/publications/identification-of-a-linear-continuous-time-stochastic-model-of-th/)\nciteturn964search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Physical knowledge as well as statistical methods are used to validate the model. ... Original language  | EnglishJournal  | Journal of Agricultural and Engineering Research ... Number of pages  | 8\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Diffusion copulas: Identification and estimation (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/343780193_Diffusion_copulas_Identification_and_estimation)\nciteturn964search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.1 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Estimation of copula-based semiparametric time series models. ... Chen, X., Hansen, L.P., Scheinkman, J., 2009.Nonlinear principal components and long run im-\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nError Analysis of an Equation Error Method for the Identification of the Diffusion Coefficient in a